<v Guy>哦，不，伙计。今晚我守海滩，宝贝。

00:40:11.880 --> 00:40:17.320
<v Kid>OK, well, I was told that this dude who makes milkshakes at the sandwich shop I go to--
<v Kid>好的，好吧，我被告知那个我在三明治店买奶昔的家伙——

00:40:17.320 --> 00:40:17.890
<v Guy>Yeah, Milky Joe?
<v Guy>是啊，奶昔乔（Milky Joe）？

00:40:17.890 --> 00:40:19.160
<v Kid>Yeah, yeah, Milky Joe.
<v Kid>是啊，是啊，奶昔乔。

00:40:19.160 --> 00:40:22.620
<v Guy>Yeah, a mix-up with Milky Joe. Milky Joe told me I'm watching the beach tonight.
<v Guy>是啊，奶昔乔搞错了。奶昔乔告诉我今晚我守海滩。

00:40:22.620 --> 00:40:24.850
<v Kid>Gotcha. Milky Joe brought me here this afternoon.
<v Kid>懂了。奶昔乔今天下午把我带到这儿的。

00:40:24.850 --> 00:40:34.980
<v Guy>Yeah, that was later than what happened with me on the other time that it did. So it was all fixed. It was so crazy. It was the other side of the time that you did it.
<v Guy>是啊，那比我上次发生的情况要晚。所以一切都解决了。这太疯狂了。那和你做的时候是同一时间。

00:40:34.980 --> 00:40:37.350
<v Kid>Oh, OK. Cool. Cool, cool.
<v Kid>哦，好的。酷。酷，酷，酷。

00:40:37.350 --> 00:40:43.040
<v Comedian>Cut to the next day. An office. There's a window out to the ocean.
<v Comedian>切到第二天。办公室。有个窗户对着海洋。

00:40:43.040 --> 00:40:48.120
<v Comedian>And there's a picture right here that says "we own the beach."
<v Comedian>那里有张画写着“海滩属于我们”。

00:40:48.120 --> 00:40:51.010
<v Boss>Hey, I want you to come look out this window, will you?
<v Boss>嘿，我要你来看看这扇窗户，行吗？

00:40:51.010 --> 00:40:51.950
<v Milky Joe>All right.
<v Milky Joe>好的。

00:40:51.950 --> 00:40:53.390
<v Boss>So you see water there?
<v Boss>你看那儿有水吗？

00:40:53.390 --> 00:40:53.750
<v Milky Joe>Yeah.
<v Milky Joe>是啊。

00:40:53.750 --> 00:40:57.340
<v Boss>You know what I'm not seeing? Any beach.
<v Boss>你知道我没看到什么吗？任何海滩。

00:40:57.340 --> 00:40:58.780
<v Milky Joe>Well, maybe someone--
<v Milky Joe>嗯，也许有人——

00:40:58.780 --> 00:41:02.880
<v Boss>I specifically told you to get someone to guard the beach last night--
<v Boss>我明确告诉你让人昨晚守海滩——

00:41:02.880 --> 00:41:03.660
<v Milky Joe>I hired--
<v Milky Joe>我雇了——

00:41:03.660 --> 00:41:05.160
<v Boss>--so that something like this wouldn't happen.
<v Boss>——这样就不会发生这种事了。

00:41:05.160 --> 00:41:08.960
<v Milky Joe>I hired two guys. I hired two guys to watch the beach.
<v Milky Joe>我雇了两个人。我雇了两个人看守海滩。

00:41:08.960 --> 00:41:13.400
<v Boss>Did you tell them that they were to work together?
<v Boss>你告诉他们要一起工作了吗？

00:41:13.400 --> 00:41:18.270
<v Milky Joe>No. Well, I hired them in opposition to one another.
<v Milky Joe>没有。嗯，我雇他们时让他们互相竞争。

00:41:18.270 --> 00:41:28.520
<v Boss>Like, I don't know how they hire security guards where you come from, but where I come from you pay somebody $12,000, they watch the beach.
<v Boss>我的意思是，我不知道你来自哪里，但在我来自的地方，你付给某人12000美元，他们就守海滩。

00:41:28.520 --> 00:41:31.680
<v Comedian>Cut to a suburban home in the basement.
<v Comedian>切到郊区家里的地下室。

00:41:31.680 --> 00:41:34.140
<v Cooper>Mom, don't come down here.
<v Cooper>妈妈，别下来。

00:41:34.140 --> 00:41:35.270
<v Mom>What? This is my house.
<v Mom>什么？这是我家。

00:41:35.270 --> 00:41:36.120
<v Cooper>Ma.
<v Cooper>妈。

00:41:36.120 --> 00:41:38.440
<v Mom>Don't tell me where I can and cannot go.
<v Mom>别告诉我我可以去哪里，不可以去哪里。

00:41:38.440 --> 00:41:40.220
<v Cooper>Fine. Just--
<v Cooper>行。只要——

00:41:40.220 --> 00:41:48.650
<v Mom>What is all of this sand? Cooper!
<v Mom>这些沙子是什么？库珀！

00:41:48.650 --> 00:41:58.360
<v Comedian>End scene.
<v Comedian>场景结束。

00:41:58.360 --> 00:42:15.980
<v Ira Glass>Mike Birbiglia, John Lutz, Kate Micucci, Shannon O'Neill, Connor Ratliff, Gary Richardson, and Tami Sagher. Mike, Kate, and Tami are three of the stars of the film that Mike and I just made called Don't Think Twice . It's not a documentary. It's a feature film. It's a comedy about a team of improv comedians and stuff that happens to them. It's in theaters now.
<v Ira Glass>迈克·比尔比利亚、约翰·卢茨、凯特·米库奇、香农·奥尼尔、康纳·拉特利夫、加里·理查森和塔米·萨格。迈克、凯特和塔米是我们刚做的那部电影《不要犹豫》（Don't Think Twice）的三位主演。它不是纪录片。是一部剧情片。这是一部关于一个即兴喜剧演员团队以及发生在他们身上的事情的喜剧。现在影院正在上映。

00:42:15.980 --> 00:42:29.740
<v Ira Glass>Coming up, a dad tries something he has never done before, one summer with his family. That's in a minute on Chicago Public Radio when our program continues.
<v Ira Glass>接下来，一位父亲尝试了他以前从未做过的事，一个与家人的夏天。那会在一分钟后的芝加哥公共广播电台继续节目。

00:42:29.740 --> 00:42:42.890
<v Ira Glass>It's This American Life . I'm Ira Glass. Each week on our program, of course, we choose a theme, bring you different stories on that theme. Today's show, My Summer Self, stories of who we are during the summer. We've arrived at Act Three of our show. Act Three, It Takes a Villa.
<v Ira Glass>这里是《美国生活》。我是艾拉·格拉斯。我们每周都会选定一个主题，带给你们关于那个主题的不同故事。今天的节目，“我的夏日自我”，讲述我们在夏天时我们是谁。我们来到了节目的第三幕。第三幕，别墅的代价。

00:42:42.890 --> 00:43:02.770
<v Ira Glass>So sometimes during the summer, people just decide they are going to try and do things they never do, go on some adventure, attempt something, and just see if it takes, see if this is who they are, see if they are the kind of summery person they have never been till now. One of our producers, Neil Drumming, witnessed his dad make an attempt like that.
<v Ira Glass>所以有时在夏天，人们只是决定他们要尝试做他们从来不做的事情，去进行某种冒险，尝试某事，看看它是否合拍，看看这是否是他们的本性，看看他们是否是那种他们直到现在才成为的夏季类型的人。我们的一位制作人，尼尔·德拉明（Neil Drumming），目睹了他的父亲做出了这样的尝试。

00:43:02.770 --> 00:43:29.140
<v Neil Drumming>In the summer of 1982, my dad did something unexpected, something that seemed unbelievably indulgent. He took me, my mom, and my brother, and the youngest of my three sisters on the most epic road trip any of us could have possibly imagined at the time. We piled into my dad's Buick Skylark and drove from Queens, New York to the World's Fair in Knoxville, Tennessee, where a robot danced for us, and then down into Orlando, Florida.
<v Neil Drumming>1982年的夏天，我父亲做了一件意想不到的事情，一件看起来令人难以置信的放纵的事。他带着我、我妈妈、我哥哥以及我三个姐妹中最小的一个，进行了当时我们所有人能想象到的最史诗级的公路旅行。我们挤进我父亲的别克天际线（Buick Skylark），从纽约皇后区开车前往田纳西州诺克斯维尔的世界博览会，那里有个机器人为我们跳舞，然后一直开到佛罗里达州的奥兰多。

00:43:29.140 --> 00:43:53.590
<v Neil Drumming>This was a big deal. Before this, going away meant visiting relatives in South Carolina and sitting uncomfortably among aunts and second cousins whose names I would forget before we'd even pulled out of their dusty driveways. This trip was not country heat and sipping sugar water on some rickety porch while listening to the inscrutable conversation of grown folk. It was what going 1,000 miles from home should feel like.
<v Neil Drumming>这可是件大事。在这之前，出门意味着去南卡罗来纳州拜访亲戚，在阿姨和堂兄弟姐妹中尴尬地坐着，他们的名字我在离开他们尘土飞扬的车道之前就会忘记。这次旅行不是乡村酷热，坐在某个摇摇欲坠的门廊上喝糖水，同时听着成年人深奥的对话。它应该是离家1000英里的感觉。

00:43:53.590 --> 00:44:07.030
<v Neil Drumming>We cruised down a brightly lit street called International Drive. We stayed at a Holiday Inn taller and more grand than any I'd ever seen. Sunlight streamed in through a hole in the ceiling, a hole that was supposed to be there.
<v Neil Drumming>我们沿着一条叫国际大道（International Drive）的灯火通明的街道行驶。我们住进了一家比我见过的任何酒店都要高大壮观的假日酒店（Holiday Inn）。阳光透过天花板上的洞洒进来，那个洞本来就应该是那样的。

00:44:07.030 --> 00:44:27.100
<v Neil Drumming>Our parents took us to a building that looked like a pile of poached eggs, but was actually Xanadu, House of the Future. And everywhere, along every roadside, billboards promised that the most magical scene still awaited us, this place, Disney World. By all accounts, it was paradise for kids.
<v Neil Drumming>我们的父母带我们去了一栋看起来像一堆荷包蛋的建筑，但实际上那是“上都”（Xanadu），未来之屋。到处都是，在每条路边，广告牌承诺着最神奇的景象仍然在等待着我们，这个地方，迪斯尼乐园（Disney World）。不管从什么方面看，对孩子来说那就是天堂。

00:44:27.100 --> 00:44:52.720
<v Neil Drumming>But between the gas and the hotels and the eating out, my dad quickly discovered how expensive taking even 60% of his brood on a Disney vacation could be. He was resigned to do it, but he wasn't above working the angles. He found out that you could get cheap tickets to the Magic Kingdom if you just signed up to sit through an hour or so spiel from someone pitching timeshares. He was in.
<v Neil Drumming>但在汽油、酒店和餐馆花销之间，我父亲很快发现带他孩子里的60%去迪斯尼度假有多贵。他不得不这样做，但他不是那种不会钻空子的人。他发现如果你只是签下参加一个小时左右推销分时度假的人的推销演说，就可以拿到去魔法王国的廉价门票。他加入了。

00:44:52.720 --> 00:45:17.770
<v Neil Drumming>The hard sell went down at the Disney Village, a branded mini mall near the famous theme park. My mom, dad, and a handful of other determined parents stowed their kids in a room full of toys that had been conveniently provided by the salespeople. The parents set about the business of listening-- or not-- waiting patiently for the moment when the closers would stop shilling and start handing out the Disney discounts.
<v Neil Drumming>强行推销发生在迪斯尼小镇（Disney Village），那个著名主题公园附近的一个品牌迷你商场。我父母和几个其他坚定的父母把孩子们关进了一个满是玩具的房间，这些玩具是销售员方便地提供的。父母们开始听这些话——或者没听——耐心地等待推销员停止推销并开始分发迪斯尼折扣的时刻。

00:45:17.770 --> 00:45:40.270
<v Neil Drumming>But while we kids were in another room throwing LEGOs at one another, something surprising happened. My dad bit. He went into a closed room to get three-day passes just so that I could eventually lose my glasses on Space Mountain. And he came out with a deed, the deed to something he and my mom were now calling our villa.
<v Neil Drumming>但当我们在另一个房间互相扔乐高积木时，发生了一些令人惊讶的事情。我父亲上钩了。他走进一个封闭的房间去买三天通票，只是为了让我最后在“太空山”（Space Mountain）丢掉眼镜。而他出来时拿着一份契据，那是一份他和我妈妈现在称之为我们“别墅”的东西的契据。

00:45:40.270 --> 00:46:00.660
<v Neil Drumming>My father was a bold man, but in retrospect this is the most impetuous action that I have ever seen him take. It cost him about $5,000, which he paid in installments. In 1982, for a guy with five kids who never made more than $33,000 a year at his day job, it was a considerable investment.
<v Neil Drumming>我父亲是个大胆的人，但回想起来，这是我见过他采取的最冲动的行动。它花费了他大约5000美元，他分期付款。在1982年，对于一个有五个孩子，在日常工作中从没赚到超过33000美元一年的人来说，这是一笔相当大的投资。

00:46:00.660 --> 00:46:25.800
<v Neil Drumming>For those unfamiliar with timeshares, it may be hard to wrap your head around buying a vacation home that you never really own. You pay upfront for it. There's an annual maintenance fee. But you only get to stay in it once a year or so, usually for a week at a time. It almost sounds like some sort of scam. And sometimes it is. But it didn't turn out that way for us. Instead, it became a fixture in my family.
<v Neil Drumming>对于那些不熟悉分时度假的人来说，可能很难理解买一个你从未真正拥有的度假屋。你预付费用。有年度维护费。但你一年只能住一次左右，通常一次住一周。听起来几乎像是某种骗局。有时确实如此。但对我们来说并非如此。相反，它成了我家庭生活的一个固定组成部分。

00:46:25.800 --> 00:46:55.800
<v Neil Drumming>My father had chosen as our week the first week in July. And so every year, during one of the hottest months of the year, we would head down I-95 as always. But now, when we pulled into South Carolina to see relatives, that was only a pit stop on the way to our true destination. We had transformed from people who went away to a family who went on vacation.
<v Neil Drumming>我父亲选定的一周是七月的第一周。所以每年，在一年中最热的月份之一，我们就像往常一样沿着95号州际公路南下。但现在，当我们驶入南卡罗来纳州看望亲戚时，那只是前往我们真正目的地的一个中转站。我们从那种“出门去的人”变成了一个“去度假的家庭”。

00:46:55.800 --> 00:47:21.900
<v Neil Drumming>Our villa was number 317, a two-bedroom apartment with an enclosed back porch that looked out onto a small man-made lake complete with fish and ducks and another summer word that I learned, gazebo. My brother chased cicadas and lizards. For my sister, the only swimmer among my siblings, there was a pool. There were tennis courts and bikes to rent. The general store even offered a collection of the latest movies on laser disc.
<v Neil Drumming>我们的别墅是317号，一套两居室公寓，有一个封闭的后廊，可以看到一个人工小湖，里面有鱼、鸭子和我学到的另一个关于夏天的词，“凉亭”。我哥哥追逐蝉和蜥蜴。对于我姐妹中唯一的游泳者，这里有一个泳池。有网球场和租用的自行车。便利店甚至提供了一系列最新的激光影碟电影。

00:47:21.900 --> 00:47:39.440
<v Neil Drumming>That first trip, I was eight. As I got older, I moved from the gazebo to the game room and then the gym, trying to meet other kids my age. My mom busied herself in the kitchen making lunches or sat by the lake and watched the ducks. My dad shepherded us through It's a Small World and Epcot Center.
<v Neil Drumming>那第一次旅行，我八岁。随着我长大，我从凉亭搬到了游戏室，然后是健身房，试着去认识其他同龄的孩子。我妈妈在厨房忙着准备午餐，或者坐在湖边看着鸭子。我父亲引导我们穿过“小小世界”（It's a Small World）和艾普科特中心（Epcot Center）。

00:47:39.440 --> 00:47:55.570
<v Neil Drumming>Our summers went on like this, pretty much exactly like this, probably until I finished high school. I honestly loved it. I looked forward to this trip every year. And even though it was only a week, it was almost always the highlight of my entire summer.
<v Neil Drumming>我们的暑假就这样继续着，几乎完全如此，直到我高中毕业。我真心喜欢它。我每年都期待这次旅行。虽然只有一周，但这几乎总是我整个暑假的亮点。

00:47:55.570 --> 00:48:11.600
<v Neil Drumming>But when I think about it now, it occurs to me my dad pretty much orchestrated this thing that became so important to our lives. And I have no idea whether or not he ever enjoyed it himself. In fact, it didn't seem like he did. I can't recall actually seeing him happy.
<v Neil Drumming>但现在想起它，我意识到我父亲几乎一手导演了这个对我们生活变得如此重要的事情。而我根本不知道他自己是否曾享受过它。事实上，看起来他并没有。我不记得曾见过他开心。

00:48:11.600 --> 00:48:27.920
<v Neil Drumming>Neither does my brother. He says dad was pretty much the same at the timeshare as he was at home. Sometimes he'd go for walks alone, but often he just sat on the couch and watched TV. I asked my sister. She said he must have been happy, but she doesn't remember witnessing it, either.
<v Neil Drumming>我哥哥也不记得。他说父亲在分时度假屋时几乎和他在家时一样。有时他会独自散步，但经常只是坐在沙发上看电视。我问了我姐妹。她说他一定很开心，但她也不记得见过。

00:48:27.920 --> 00:48:40.110
<v Neil Drumming>It seems like such a simple question, but I just wanted to know. Did he enjoy himself?
<v Neil Drumming>这似乎是个简单的问题，但我只是想知道。他自己享受吗？

00:48:40.110 --> 00:49:01.410
<v Neil Drumming>At the risk of embodying the most tired trope in all of modern masculinity, I will say my father and I never really got along. He was strict. His house had a lot of rules. And he believed in corporal punishment. And the sting of that conflict stayed with me as an adult. But since my mom passed away last year, I've been trying to connect with him more. I gave him a call.
<v Neil Drumming>冒着体现现代男子气概中最陈旧比喻的风险，我要说我父亲和我从没真正合得来。他很严厉。他的家有很多规则。他相信体罚。那种冲突的刺痛感一直伴随我到成年。但自从我妈妈去年去世后，我一直试图和他多联系。我给他打了个电话。

00:49:01.410 --> 00:49:05.370
<v Neil Drumming>[TELEPHONE RINGING]
<v Neil Drumming>[电话铃响]

00:49:05.370 --> 00:49:06.350
<v Dad>Hello?
<v Dad>喂？

00:49:06.350 --> 00:49:07.490
<v Neil Drumming>Hello?
<v Neil Drumming>喂？

00:49:07.490 --> 00:49:08.250
<v Dad>Yes?
<v Dad>是？

00:49:08.250 --> 00:49:09.330
<v Neil Drumming>Hey, it's Neil.
<v Neil Drumming>嘿，是尼尔（Neil）。

00:49:09.330 --> 00:49:10.340
<v Dad>Yes.
<v Dad>是。

00:49:10.340 --> 00:49:11.500
<v Neil Drumming>Is it a bad time?
<v Neil Drumming>现在不方便吗？

00:49:11.500 --> 00:49:12.730
<v Dad>It's about who?
<v Dad>是关于谁？

00:49:12.730 --> 00:49:14.460
<v Neil Drumming>I say, is it a bad time?
<v Neil Drumming>我说，现在不方便吗？

00:49:14.460 --> 00:49:22.640
<v Dad>No. No, I was just playing solitaire. Yeah. I didn't know whether it was the drugstore or not.
<v Dad>不。不，我只是在玩纸牌。是啊。我不知道那是药店还是别的地方。

00:49:22.640 --> 00:49:24.720
<v Neil Drumming>Are you waiting for a call from the drugstore?
<v Neil Drumming>你在等药店的电话吗？

00:49:24.720 --> 00:49:29.940
<v Dad>No. They'll call. They'll give me a call no matter when it is.
<v Dad>不。他们会打的。不管什么时候他们都会打给我。

00:49:29.940 --> 00:49:44.740
<v Neil Drumming>My dad is 83 years old now and living alone in Florida. Talking to him can be awkward and not just because his hearing is going. I asked him point blank if he liked going to the villa. He told me that when he was growing up, he barely ever left South Carolina.
<v Neil Drumming>我父亲现在83岁了，独自住在佛罗里达。和他谈话可能会很尴尬，不仅是因为他的听力在衰退。我直截了当地问他是否喜欢去那栋别墅。他告诉我，在他成长过程中，他几乎没离开过南卡罗来纳州。

00:49:44.740 --> 00:50:11.680
<v Dad>I didn't know nothing about nothing else. You know, like, you saw things in magazines and stuff. The first time I knew about a dentist I was in the army. But I just thought it was a good idea that our kids see something other than their surroundings and where they were born.
<v Dad>我对其他事一无所知。你知道，就像，你在杂志之类的东西里看到东西。我第一次知道什么是牙医是在军队里。但我只是认为让我们的孩子看到除了他们周围环境以及他们出生地之外的东西是个好主意。

00:50:11.680 --> 00:50:13.370
<v Neil Drumming>Yeah.
<v Neil Drumming>是啊。

00:50:13.370 --> 00:50:30.190
<v Ira Glass>My dad grew up poor on a farm, one of 12 children. He says he only finished high school because by the time he was old enough, he was the one driving the bus. Sometimes when there were athletic events at other schools, he'd get to drive the teams and learn what the nearby towns were like.
<v Ira Glass>我父亲在农场贫穷的环境中长大，12个孩子中的一个。他说他只读完了高中，因为在他够大的时候，他就是那个开校车的人。有时当其他学校有体育赛事时，他会去开校车带球队，了解附近的小镇是什么样的。

00:50:30.190 --> 00:50:43.340
<v Ira Glass>In 1953, he was drafted into the army, which had only recently been integrated. They sent him to Colorado and Indiana. And it wasn't great. He says the army was really not into black people back then.
<v Ira Glass>1953年，他被征召入伍，当时军队才刚刚废除种族隔离不久。他们把他派往科罗拉多和印第安纳。情况并不好。他说那时的军队真的很不喜欢黑人。

00:50:43.340 --> 00:50:52.020
<v Ira Glass>So those were his travel experiences when he was young. I was hearing a lot of this for the first time. As it turns out, that's at least partially my own fault.
<v Ira Glass>所以这就是他年轻时的旅行经历。我很多时候是第一次听到这些。事实证明，至少部分是因为我自己的错。

00:50:52.020 --> 00:51:00.280
<v Dad>The reason why we never talk about it because it just wasn't the kind of thing that you guys seemed to be interested in.
<v Dad>我们从不谈论它的原因是因为这似乎不是你们感兴趣的那种事。

00:51:00.280 --> 00:51:04.720
<v Neil Drumming>Really? So we just didn't seem like we were interested as kids?
<v Neil Drumming>真的吗？所以我们小时候看起来对这些不感兴趣？

00:51:04.720 --> 00:51:05.940
<v Dad>Yeah. Right.
<v Dad>是啊。对。

00:51:05.940 --> 00:51:21.490
<v Neil Drumming>Yeah, I probably wasn't so interested back then, back when the two of us were constantly challenging each other. I was always either afraid of him or angry at him, hiding from him or planting my feet to confront him. It never crossed my mind to try to understand him.
<v Neil Drumming>是啊，在那时我们经常相互挑战的时候，我可能没那么感兴趣。我要么怕他，要么生他的气，躲着他或者站稳脚跟和他对抗。我从来没想过要去尝试了解他。

00:51:21.490 --> 00:51:39.390
<v Neil Drumming>But nowadays my dad feels to me like some kind of living cold case, a million-page brief that is no longer redacted. Maybe it's because I'm now at the age he was when I was born, but I retroactively find his every decision fascinating, even the ones that aren't so surprising on the surface.
<v Neil Drumming>但如今我父亲对我来说感觉像某种活着悬而未决的案件，一份不再被涂黑的百万页简报。也许是因为我现在到了他生我时的年龄，所以我追溯性地觉得他每一个决定都很迷人，即使是那些表面上看起来并不令人惊讶的决定。

00:51:39.390 --> 00:51:41.980
<v Neil Drumming>Why Florida?
<v Neil Drumming>为什么是佛罗里达？

00:51:41.980 --> 00:52:02.460
<v Dad>It was advertised. And you get to hear something about Florida. And then this thing, Disney World. After we started going, they build Epcot. They build Animal Kingdom. And they advertise them a lot.
<v Dad>那是广告宣传的。而且你开始听到一些关于佛罗里达的事。然后这东西，迪斯尼世界。在我们开始去之后，他们建造了艾普科特。他们建造了动物王国。他们做了很多宣传。

00:52:02.460 --> 00:52:17.810
<v Dad>Not many people were going. We were probably the most vacationing people in our area. I don't know of any other family that went on vacation every year. We did.
<v Dad>没多少人去。我们可能是我们那个地区最常度假的人了。我不知道有任何其他家庭每年去度假。我们做了。

00:52:17.810 --> 00:52:45.580
<v Neil Drumming>My dad was obviously proud that he got the timeshare. But pride, strictly speaking, does not constitute joy. It didn't answer the question of whether or not he was actually happy spending those summer weeks with us at the villa. Instead, he kept trying to make me understand why he brought us there in the first place. And his explanation, his reasoning, reached back to memories and past experiences that not only had I never heard, but that kind of blew my mind.
<v Neil Drumming>我父亲显然为他拿到分时度假权而自豪。但严格来说，骄傲并不等于快乐。它没有回答他是否真的在别墅和我们共度那些夏日周时感到快乐。相反，他不断试图让我理解他最初为什么把我们带到那里。他的解释，他的推理，回溯到不仅是我从未听过，而且让我大开眼界的记忆和过去的经历。

00:52:45.580 --> 00:53:32.800
<v Dad>Now, I tell you, probably where I got the whole idea, when we went to school, every summer you had to try to think of something that you could write about when you go back to school because you're going to have to write something about what you did this summer. Well, we never had anything to write about when I was going to school. And you didn't think plowing a mule, or picking peaches or stuff, that you had to normally do, you didn't think that was so exciting to write about. And so we made up lies about what we did.
<v Dad>现在，我告诉你，大概我从哪儿得来的这个主意，当我们上学时，每年夏天你必须试着想一些东西写下来，当你回到学校时，因为你回到学校后必须写点关于你夏天做了什么。嗯，当我上学时我们从来没有任何可以写的东西。而且你并不认为耕骡子，或者摘桃子之类，那些你通常必须要做的事，你并不认为那是那么令人兴奋可以写出来的。所以我们编造了关于我们做了什么的谎言。

00:53:32.800 --> 00:53:44.320
<v Dad>Well, every summer you guys went on vacation, you could write about something that you did or saw or some place you went.
<v Dad>嗯，每年夏天你们去度假时，你们可以写点你们做的或者看到的，或者你们去的什么地方。

00:53:44.320 --> 00:53:49.800
<v Neil Drumming>Yeah. What did you do during the summers?
<v Neil Drumming>是啊。你夏天做了什么？

00:53:49.800 --> 00:53:52.070
<v Dad>When? This year?
<v Dad>什么时候？今年？

00:53:52.070 --> 00:53:55.840
<v Neil Drumming>No, no, when you were in school.
<v Neil Drumming>不，不，当你上学时。

00:53:55.840 --> 00:54:09.250
<v Dad>Worked. That's what I tried to tell you.
<v Dad>工作。这就是我试着告诉你的。

00:54:09.250 --> 00:54:19.390
<v Neil Drumming>We talked for over an hour. It was one of the longest conversations that I can remember us ever having. Every now and then, I try to steer him back to the question I wanted him to answer.
<v Neil Drumming>我们谈了一个多小时。这是我记得我们之间进行过的最长的谈话之一。时不时地，我试着引导他回到我想要他回答的问题上。

00:54:19.390 --> 00:54:28.660
<v Neil Drumming>So I know I asked you this a bunch of times. I keep asking you the same question. You can tell me to stop asking you if you want, but did you have fun yourself?
<v Neil Drumming>所以我知道我问过你很多次了。我一直问你同一个问题。如果你想让我停下来问你，你可以告诉我不问了，但你自己玩得开心吗？

00:54:28.660 --> 00:55:02.880
<v Dad>Yeah. See, I don't regret anything because it looked to me like I was doing what I was supposed to do. And to see your kids happy was to be happy, too. And you guys could always come in and do whatever it is and go back out to the pool or whatever. I remember you guys playing out there and hanging around the bushes and stuff. I thought it was great.
<v Dad>是啊。你看，我不后悔任何事，因为对我来说，看起来我做了我应该做的事。看到你们孩子快乐，我也就快乐了。你们总是可以进来做任何事，然后回到泳池或任何地方。我记得你们在外面玩耍，在灌木丛周围转悠之类的。我觉得那太棒了。

00:55:02.880 --> 00:55:26.020
<v Neil Drumming>That's the kind of enjoyment I hadn't considered. I live more selfishly. Also, his answer was hard to take in, to reconcile with the distance I felt between us at the time, back when he would retire to the couch to watch TV while we went off to play on our own. Maybe he was watching me play in the bushes and getting a kick out of it. But I didn't know that.
<v Neil Drumming>那是种我没考虑过的享受。我活得更自私。而且，他的回答很难接受，很难与当时我感到的我们之间的疏离感调和，那时候他会回到沙发上看电视，而我们去自己玩耍。也许他在看着我在灌木丛里玩耍并从中得到快乐。但我不知道。

00:55:26.020 --> 00:55:32.310
<v Neil Drumming>Still, I was happy at the villa. And my dad says he was, too. I'm glad I know that.
<v Neil Drumming>不过，我在别墅里很开心。我父亲说他也是。我很高兴我知道这点。

00:55:32.310 --> 00:55:51.220
<v Neil Drumming>All right. So I've been talking to you for an hour. I should probably let you go. But, hey, is it OK if I call back this week and just talk? I want to hear more stuff. Since I didn't seem interested when I was a kid, I didn't realize that was why you didn't tell us stuff. So now I'll just ask. Is that OK if I could--
<v Neil Drumming>好的。所以我已经和你聊了一个小时了。我大概应该让你走了。但，嘿，我这个星期可以再打来只是聊聊吗？我想听更多的事。因为我小时候看起来不感兴趣，我没意识到那就是你没告诉我们事的原因。所以现在我会问。如果我可以——这样行吗？

00:55:51.220 --> 00:56:05.340
<v Dad>The only thing I'd do is to get up and sometimes I'm outside just walking around. Sometimes I sit down and-- sometimes I go ride the bike. And I do this just to keep busy. You can call me anytime--
<v Dad>我唯一会做的事就是起床，有时我在外面只是四处走走。有时我坐下来，而且——有时我去骑自行车。我这样做只是为了让自己有事做。你可以随时打电话给我——

00:56:05.340 --> 00:56:06.940
<v Neil Drumming>All right. I'm going to go back to work.
<v Neil Drumming>好的。我要回去工作了。

00:56:06.940 --> 00:56:07.310
<v Dad>OK.
<v Dad>好的。

00:56:07.310 --> 00:56:08.050
<v Neil Drumming>Bye, Dad.
<v Neil Drumming>再见，爸爸。

00:56:08.050 --> 00:56:15.130
<v Dad>Bye.
<v Dad>再见。

00:56:15.130 --> 00:56:17.900
<v Ira Glass>Neil Drumming, one of the producers of our show.
<v Ira Glass>尼尔·德拉明，我们节目的制作人之一。

00:56:17.900 --> 00:56:30.180
